<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SHRUBLAND_GIANT_ENRAGE_1. The scoped, time-boxed rage a "giant with young"
  race drops into when something walks up to its young.

  design/Jawa/worldbuilding/biomes/arid_shrubland.md §4, the owner-ratified
  size ladder: "Large - giants' children only, and approach is attack: the
  parent enrages if you even get near the young. No warning is given."

  RM tier: names no species. Any race carrying
  RimMandrake.CreatureBehaviors.RM_ParentalEnrageExtension points its
  enrageState at this def; nothing here knows what a giant is.

  === Why there is no think-tree patch anywhere in this build ===

  stateClass derives from vanilla MentalState_Manhunter, and Core's own
  ThinkTreeDef MentalStateNonCritical routes manhunting with
  ThinkNode_ConditionalMentalStateClass, whose Satisfied() is
  `stateClass.IsInstanceOfType(mentalState)` : an INSTANCE check, not the
  def-identity check its sibling ThinkNode_ConditionalMentalState performs.
  So a subclass inherits JobGiver_Manhunter's whole chase-and-melee behaviour
  for free and no vanilla def is patched at all. MEASURED against the
  decompiled 1.6 engine, 2026-09-20 (Verse/AI/
  ThinkNode_ConditionalMentalStateClass.cs l.19, and MentalStateNonCritical's
  own manhunter node). Losing that inheritance would leave a pawn holding a
  mental state with no behaviour attached, which is silent, so the subclass
  relationship is load-bearing and must not be flattened.

  === No ParentName on purpose ===

  Vanilla's Manhunter inherits BaseMentalState, which supplies exactly
  minTicksBeforeRecovery and recoveryMtbDays, both of which this def has to
  override anyway to be SHORT. Declaring them here instead of inheriting them
  removes an outward-pointing name and the whole load-order failure class that
  comes with it.

  === Deliberately no beginLetter ===

  "No warning is given" is the ruling. The charge IS the notification. A
  begin-letter would be precisely the warning the biome sheet says the player
  does not get, so the absence of one here is content, not an omission.

  Timing: minTicksBeforeRecovery 600 (~15 in-game minutes) is the floor, and
  the recoveryMtbDays roll can end it any time after that; on top of both,
  RM_CompParentalEnrage sets MentalState.forceRecoverAfterTicks from the
  race's own extension (2500 ticks on the shrubland giant), and
  RM_MentalState_ParentalEnrage ends it the moment the intruder backs off,
  dies, goes down or leaves. Vanilla Manhunter's own floor is 10000 ticks;
  being far under that is the "time-boxed, not a manhunter flip" half of the
  item.
-->
<Defs>

  <MentalStateDef>
    <defName>RM_ParentalEnrage</defName>
    <label>defending young</label>
    <stateClass>RimMandrake.CreatureBehaviors.RM_MentalState_ParentalEnrage</stateClass>
    <!-- Aggro is what puts the pawn into AttackTargetsCache's
         pawnsInAggroMentalState set and makes Pawn.InAggroMentalState true,
         so the game treats it as a live threat while it lasts. -->
    <category>Aggro</category>
    <baseInspectLine>Enraged: defending young</baseInspectLine>
    <recoveryMessage>{0} has stopped defending its young.</recoveryMessage>
    <nameColor>(0.9,0.2,0.5)</nameColor>
    <minTicksBeforeRecovery>600</minTicksBeforeRecovery>
    <recoveryMtbDays>0.05</recoveryMtbDays>
    <stopsJobs>true</stopsJobs>
    <recoverFromDowned>true</recoverFromDowned>
    <!-- A grazing herd sleeps; a sleeping guardian that woke for this must
         not instantly drop the state again on the next tick. -->
    <recoverFromSleep>false</recoverFromSleep>
    <inCaravanCanDo>false</inCaravanCanDo>
  </MentalStateDef>

</Defs>
